---
import GovernanceDocument from '~/components/ui/GovernanceDocument.astro';
import { getPermalink } from '~/utils/permalinks';

const sections = [
  { id: 'what-bylaws-do', title: 'What bylaws do' },
  { id: 'how-hta-is-organized', title: 'How HTA is organized' },
  { id: 'board-decisions', title: 'How the board makes decisions' },
  { id: 'financial-stewardship', title: 'Money, conflicts, and community trust' },
  { id: 'changes-and-questions', title: 'Changes and governance questions' },
];
---

<GovernanceDocument
  page="bylaws"
  title="Bylaws"
  category="Governance"
  description="A plain-language guide to Heartland Transpersonal Alliance’s current consolidated bylaws draft: who makes decisions, what the board oversees, and how it is held accountable."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="what-bylaws-do" aria-labelledby="what-bylaws-do-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="what-bylaws-do-heading">What bylaws do</h2>
    <p>
      Bylaws set out how a nonprofit governs itself. They determine who has authority, how the board makes decisions,
      and how the organization changes its rules. The <a href={getPermalink('/code-of-conduct')}>ethical code</a>
      addresses a different question: how people should act and what to do when someone raises a concern.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page summarizes the current consolidated draft. It is not the signed bylaws or a record of formal adoption.
      For a question that depends on the exact wording or legal status, contact HTA.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-hta-is-organized" aria-labelledby="how-hta-is-organized-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="how-hta-is-organized-heading">How HTA is organized</h2>
    <p>
      Heartland Transpersonal Alliance is a Missouri charitable and educational nonprofit. The Psychedelic Society of
      Kansas City, Safe Journey Sanctum, the Kansas City Psychedelic Integration Circle, and the Kansas City Psychedelic
      Conference operate under HTA’s board oversight. Programs may not make commitments on HTA’s behalf without
      authorization.
    </p>
    <p>
      The draft describes a board-governed organization with at least three directors. Participating in a program,
      donating, or volunteering does not confer legal voting rights in the corporation. HTA may invite community input
      without transferring the board’s governing authority.
    </p>
    <p>
      The board oversees strategy, finances, and programs. The president leads the board; the executive director leads
      operations and programs; the secretary keeps board records; and the treasurer oversees budgets, financial
      reporting, and filings. Committees work within a written scope and delegated authority. They report back to the
      board, which remains responsible for oversight.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="board-decisions" aria-labelledby="board-decisions-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="board-decisions-heading">How the board makes decisions</h2>
    <p>
      Under the draft, a majority of the directors in office is needed to hold a vote. At a meeting with that quorum,
      ordinary decisions generally require a majority of directors present. Directors can meet remotely. Action outside
      a meeting requires unanimous written consent. Board minutes record decisions, votes, and relevant conflict
      disclosures.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="financial-stewardship" aria-labelledby="financial-stewardship-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="financial-stewardship-heading">Money, conflicts, and community trust</h2>
    <p>
      The draft calls for budgets, financial records, documented approvals, and honoring donor restrictions. Directors
      disclose personal, family, financial, or business conflicts, do not vote on the affected matter, and have the
      disclosure recorded. Disinterested directors review compensation involving insiders.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also calls for consent and safety, lawful harm reduction, nondiscrimination, privacy in handling records, and
      no retaliation for good-faith reporting or participation in an investigation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="changes-and-questions" aria-labelledby="changes-and-questions-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="changes-and-questions-heading">Changes and governance questions</h2>
    <p>
      The draft sets a two-thirds vote of directors in office for amendments, with notice and documentation, unless a
      higher threshold applies. If HTA dissolves, any assets remaining after its obligations are met are to go to
      qualifying charitable purposes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={getPermalink('/contact')}>Contact HTA</a> to ask about the governing document or send a governance question.
      You can also <a href={getPermalink('/board')}>meet the Board of Directors</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</GovernanceDocument>
